23:13:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: To get a plumber to follow a complicated warranty, you need to translate the fine print into a short, job-site-ready checklist. Plumbers care about **what they must physically do**, **what paperwork to fill out**, and **what will get the warranty claim rejected**. Everything else is noise. Here’s how to simplify it down to exactly what he’ll follow: ### 1. Do the heavy lifting yourself first Read the full warranty and highlight only the clauses that require an action from the installer. Ignore customer obligations (like paying bills) and legal definitions. Focus on three questions: - What must be done during installation? (e.g. flushing, filter type, pipe sizing) - What must be documented? (e.g. Benchmark book, commissioning report) - What must be done yearly? (service intervals, by whom) ### 2. Strip out legalese and write in “installer speak” Replace phrases like “system must be cleansed in accordance with BS 7593” with “Powerflush the system and add inhibitor — dose it and log the reading.” Never just hand him the warranty booklet; his eyes will glaze over. Give him a single sheet with bullet points. ### 3. Create a one-page “Warranty Conditions for Installer” sheet Use a template like this, filling in the specifics from your boiler’s warranty: --- **BOILER WARRANTY — INSTALLER MUST-DO’S** *All steps below must be done or the manufacturer will not pay out a claim.* **ON INSTALL DAY:** - [ ] **Magnetic filter** fitted on the return pipe (model X or equivalent) - [ ] System flushed with a **powerflush machine** (chemical only is not enough) - [ ] **Inhibitor** added: product name ________, dose ________, reading on test kit ________ ppm - [ ] **Gas supply** pipe sized correctly: minimum 22 mm from meter to boiler - [ ] **Condensate pipe** run internally or insulated to outside with 32 mm fall - [ ] Complete the **Benchmark commissioning checklist** while firing the boiler (not from memory later) - [ ] Record **gas inlet pressure** and **CO/CO₂ readings** — values must be within: X–Y mbar / Z% - [ ] Take a photo of the filled Benchmark book and the serial number label — send to me (the customer) on the day **ANNUAL SERVICE (to keep warranty valid):** - Must be done by a Gas Safe registered engineer - Service must follow the manufacturer’s checklist in the manual (not a quick look-over) - Record service in the Benchmark service log, stamp and sign each year - Keep the printed service report for your records. If the boiler breaks down, the manufacturer will ask for it. **THINGS THAT INSTANTLY VOID THE WARRANTY:** - No magnetic filter - Benchmarked book not fully completed on day of installation - No proof of annual service (even one missed year) - Use of non-manufacturer parts on any safety or burner component - Pipework sized incorrectly causing low flow (manufacturer can check data logs) --- ### 4. Use pictures and a physical checklist, not a paragraph Print that one-page sheet and hook it to the boiler or leave it with the Benchmark book. On site, walk him through the 5 on-the-day items and say: *“If these 5 aren’t ticked and photo’d, the warranty is worthless, so I’ll need that photo before you pack up.”* That frames it as a simple job requirement, not a lecture. ### 5. Frame it as protecting his callback, not your paperwork Plumbers hate warranty callbacks they don’t get paid for. Tell him: *“I’m not trying to tell you how to do your job — I just don’t want you to get a call in six months from a manufacturer refusing the claim because a box wasn’t ticked. This sheet makes sure they can’t blame you.”* ### 6. Confirm the “benchmark moment” Simply ask: *“Just to be clear, you will fill out the Benchmark in front of me and text me a photo of it complete, plus the serial number?”* If he says yes, you’ve got your commitment. If he says “I’ll do it in the van later,” push back — incomplete Benchmark logs are the #1 rejected warranty reason. By reducing the entire warranty to a 10-bullet installer checklist and insisting on photographic proof of the Benchmark, you remove ambiguity and make compliance the path of least resistance.
